# aql — working todo

Scratch list. The structured version is ROADMAP.md; this is the running one.

## direction (see ARCHITECTURE.md)
- Aql = one hub that owns everything physical. Seven device kinds: camera, lighting,
  robot, climate, energy, sensor, access. Access is the most complete and the only one
  that does NOT go through the device engine — it is still a parallel stack.
- Chat is an input surface across the hub, not the product.
- Shipped: the hub (open path, console, audit, SQLite), the controller agent
  (pinned-key pairing, fail-closed verification, offline LAN grants), signed commands,
  proto/'s conformance vectors, the e2e harnesses, the device engine + four drivers,
  the automations runtime, energy metering, and the console screens over all of it.

## naming, settled
- The hub is a **hub**. Never "the gateway" in prose — in the KOTVA family that word is
  the legacy-rail coordinator role, which is Ephor's job. The directory is `hub/`.
- Still spelled the old way, on purpose, as compat surface: `LINTEL_*` env vars (read
  behind `lookupEnv`, with a startup warning), `lintel.db`, `_lintel._tcp`,
  `lintel.offline-access` (an IndexedDB rename orphans rather than migrates), and
  proto/vectors/. Guarded by src/lib/__tests__/naming.test.ts.

## next up
- [ ] BLE radio validation on real hardware — framing and session are tested, the radio
      has never run. `-tags ble` builds a real GATT server on Linux (BlueZ) AND Windows
      (WinRT); only darwin and below get ErrUnsupported from
      `bleperiph/start_unsupported.go`, and that one is blocked upstream:
      tinygo.org/x/bluetooth v0.15.0 has no GATT server for darwin at all
      (gatts_other.go is a nine-line stub), so macOS is a BLE central only.
- [x] TestAFailedPersistRollsBackEveryField is now root-proof. It simulated a disk
      failure with `chmod 0500`, which the kernel does not enforce against uid 0, so
      under `docker run` the persist it expected to fail SUCCEEDED and it reported a
      rollback defect that did not exist. It now replaces the state directory with a
      REGULAR FILE so the create fails ENOTDIR — enforced for everyone. Verified on real
      Linux as root AND as an ordinary user, and it still catches the original
      shared-Pairing-pointer bug (confirmed by reintroducing exactly that).
      GitHub's runners are non-root, so CI was green throughout; the bite is containers,
      including this repo's own docker build.
- LESSON: a permission-based failure simulation is a no-op for root. It failed loudly in
      the direction we hit, but the dangerous direction is silent — a fail-closed test
      whose refusal is simulated by chmod would PASS VACUOUSLY as root. This was the only
      os.Chmod in any test in either module; there are now none.
- CORRECTION: the commit that carried this fix (09366f8) also added a CI step to run the
      tagged tests, on the stated premise that "no CI run had ever executed one of them".
      THAT WAS FALSE. Two steps already did: the race job's
      `go test -race -tags gpio|ble ./...`, and the controller job's `go test (optional
      backends)` — the latter byte-for-byte what was added, directly below a comment
      explaining that this exact gap had already been closed. The step has been reverted.
- [x] The suites have now RUN on arm64 Linux, which is what a Pi is and what the release
      workflow ships as `linux/arm64 (Pi)`. Every CI job is ubuntu-latest/amd64, so the
      arm64 binaries were cross-compiled and never executed. Done in docker on an Apple
      Silicon host (native aarch64, not emulated), as ROOT, which is a second axis CI
      never covers either: hub 17/17 packages, exit 0; controller full suite plus
      `-tags gpio` under `-race`, clean; the GPIO kernel-ABI struct sizes and field
      offsets pass; e2e ok. No defect found — this is a confirmation, not a fix.
- NOTE: an arm64 CI job was considered and REJECTED, because the repo already solves this
      better than a runtime job would. gpio_abi_static.go asserts every kernel struct size
      and field offset as a CONSTANT EXPRESSION, so the existing cross-compile checks the
      layout on arm64, arm AND 386 — including 32-bit arm, where Go aligns uint64 to 4
      bytes rather than 8 and a struct correct on amd64 can genuinely differ. A runtime
      job would cost an emulator or a runner and cover fewer architectures. The comment in
      that file already named the gap I thought I had found.
- ROOT CAUSE, worth more than the incident: the search that "found no CI reference" was
      piped through `head -10`. It had 34 matches and every ci.yml line sat at rank 27+.
      A truncated search does not report absence, it reports the first N — and it looks
      identical to a complete one. `head` on a search whose ANSWER is "does X appear
      anywhere" is the bug; count first, or drop the pipe.
- [ ] GPIO relay validation on real hardware — the driver exists behind `-tags gpio` and
      has never driven a chip, a relay board or a gate. Fail-safe spec: normally-open,
      line drops on exit/panic. The uAPI layout IS now verified on linux/arm64, arm and
      386 at compile time (gpio_abi_static.go), so what remains is genuinely the radio
      and the relay board, not the struct definitions.
- LESSON: CI built the tagged backends but never TESTED them. `go test ./...` cannot see
      a file behind //go:build gpio, so three test files had never run in the pipeline.
      A tagged backend needs both a build step and a test step.
- [x] Present an offline grant over LAN from a browser. The controller now allows the
      ONE origin of the hub console it paired with (lanserver/cors.go), so an
      http-served console can complete the handshake. Desktop shell unchanged.
- [ ] Present over LAN from an HTTPS console — blocked by mixed content, not by CORS,
      and no header fixes it. Would need the controller to serve TLS on the LAN, which
      means a cert for a .local name. Open question, not a task yet.
- [ ] Present over BLE from a browser — genuinely impossible; needs native code in
      src-tauri (also the only way to resolve .local reliably). Tracked as the
      offline-grant-app-client claim.
- [x] Access now APPEARS in the device engine: hub/internal/devices/accessdev, wired as
      `-device-drivers=access`, the only driver needing no -device-config because it reads
      the DB. Status-only capability, Execute refuses every verb in the catalogue.
- [x] The console question (§5) is closed too. Devices.tsx already linked out rather than
      duplicating controls; what the fold broke was that the row list is a CONCATENATION,
      so every gate rendered twice once the driver was on. Engine-sourced access rows are
      now suppressed — the row, not the device: /v1/engine/devices still returns gates.
      DESIGNED: docs/ACCESS-ON-THE-ENGINE.md. Decisions: actuation
      does NOT move (read-only driver; two actuation routes to a gate is strictly worse
      than one), source of truth is the DB not the config file, ids are
      `access:<access_point_id>`, the engine writes no audit row.
- NOTE: the original deferral in devices/driver.go was correct and its premise has
      EXPIRED. It said the fold would prove "a seam that has no other drivers yet"; the
      seam now has four, and the generic ones carry a kind they were not written for.
- [x] Step 1 of that plan is DONE and is the part worth having regardless: a test pinning
      every access verb's tier above MaxActionTier. Today an automation cannot open a gate
      for TWO reasons — access is not in the registry at all, AND the tier is above the
      ceiling. The fold removes the first. That test is what pays for it, and it is
      written BEFORE the fold on purpose: a compensating control added alongside the thing
      it compensates for is one review away from being dropped as noise.
- [ ] A DEDICATED robot driver. The kind is reachable today through the generic HTTP and
      MQTT drivers — kind robot, robot.job/robot.blade-job, one action per verb, with
      TierHazardousMotion intact (tested in devices/httpdev). What is missing is vendor
      integration: a mower own protocol, docking, scheduling. No robot has been driven.

## energy rollups and the timezone trap
- [x] The hub now WARNS at startup when metering runs with AQL_ENERGY_TZ unset, and
      hub/README.md says what the default costs. Rollup buckets anchor to UTC by default,
      so every daily and monthly total splits at UTC midnight — wrong for most of the
      world, and wrong in the way nobody notices: right shape, right magnitudes, only the
      boundaries off.
- NOTE: the reason this needed more than a doc line is that the mistake HARDENS. `tz` is
      part of energy_rollups' primary key, every read filters on the current zone, and
      rollups are recomputed only from the dirty queue ingest marks — "reads never do this
      work" is a deliberate design property, not an oversight. So switching zones after
      months of running leaves the old buckets correct and permanently invisible.
- [x] The remedy is BUILT: `aql-hub energy rebucket -account ID [-tz ZONE] [-dry-run]`.
      Marks the retained span dirty under the current zone and drains the ordinary rollup
      path, so the history is rebuilt from the raw samples. Additive and idempotent — it
      queues work rather than writing rollups, leaves the previous zone's buckets exactly
      where they are (deleting them would turn a recoverable mistake into an unrecoverable
      one if the NEW zone were also wrong), and an interrupted run is finished by re-running.
      Round-trip tested: ingest under UTC, switch to Africa/Johannesburg, watch the series
      go empty, rebuild, watch it come back — with the UTC buckets still intact.
- NOTE: it still cannot recover everything and the command says so BEFORE it starts, not
      after. Rollups are rebuilt from samples; samples are pruned on
      AQL_ENERGY_SAMPLE_RETENTION (30 days default). Older than the oldest surviving
      sample is gone in every zone. A recovery tool that prints a success line over a
      partial result is worse than one that refuses, because the operator stops looking.

## camera: the one seam left, and why it stays
- [ ] Nothing joins RTSP to a written clip. camera tests the wire (real in-process RTSP
      server) and stops at access units; recording tests access-units-to-clip against a
      FetchFunc FAKE. Both sides of the contract are now pinned — camera asserts
      ConsumeMedia surfaces in-band SPS/PPS and keeps them out of the samples, recording
      asserts CaptureOnce writes a clip given those — but the fake still decides what the
      real one would return.
- NOTE: closing it needs the fake RTSP server extracted into an importable package, because
      recording imports camera so camera cannot import recording. MEASURED: 23 call sites
      across 2 files set 24 unexported fields directly, so extraction means exporting all
      of them or writing setters, in the tests that guard the camera wire path. Judged
      disproportionate to the residual risk. Recorded as a decision, not an oversight —
      it had been deferred twice without one.

## damage paths on critical state files (complete)
- NOTE: every file the hub or controller loads at boot has an ABSENT case (create it) and
      a DAMAGED case (refuse, or fail closed). The two must not be confused: generating on
      damage is silent, and what it costs depends entirely on the file.
- [x] gateway_ed25519.seed — refuses, does not re-key, does not rewrite the damaged bytes.
      Regenerating would break PAIRING: every controller pins this key, so every command
      and every offline grant would be rejected while the hub looked healthy.
- [x] gateway_ed25519.prev (retained key) — already covered in keys/rotation_test.go.
- [x] jwt_secret — refuses, and the absent case persists so the next boot reads it rather
      than minting another. Regenerating only invalidates SESSIONS: everyone logged out,
      silently, on an otherwise clean restart. Less severe than the signing key and worth
      distinguishing rather than treating as the same problem.
- [x] controller state.json — two DIFFERENT deliberate answers, both already tested:
      malformed JSON refuses to start (`state: corrupt state.json`), while a malformed
      pinned KEY inside valid JSON starts and fails closed on verification, so the device
      can still be reached and repaired rather than being bricked at a gate.
- NOTE: verified end to end on the shipped container as well — a corrupt seed exits 1 with
      a message naming the file, and the signing key is stable across restarts.

## concurrency coverage (partial)
- NOTE: `go test -race` only reports races on paths that ACTUALLY run in parallel during
      the test. A package whose tests are single-threaded gets a green race run that means
      nothing, and the race CI job covering all three modules reads as far stronger
      assurance than it is.
- NOTE: e2e does not close this. `go test -race` instruments the TEST process; e2e spawns
      the hub and controller as SUBPROCESSES, so races inside those binaries are invisible
      to it however concurrent the traffic is.
- [x] internal/recording (the live-view Broadcaster) and internal/hub (the device
      registry, on the path every signed open takes to a gate). Both came through clean;
      both are now pinned by tampers that reproduce the specific hazard.
- [x] controller/internal/noncestore — and this one was not just uncovered, it had a
      latent REPLAY window. Seen() and Mark() lock separately, and command verification
      checks early (so a lockdown refusal does not burn the nonce) then records on
      acceptance. Across two lock acquisitions, two verifications of the same envelope
      both pass and both actuate. Demonstrated: the old pair admitted 30 of 32 concurrent
      redemptions. Closed with MarkIfUnseen, an atomic check-and-record; exactly one now.
- NOTE: NOT reachable today — the transport runs one goroutine and the long-poll fallback
      only runs after the WebSocket session returns. The point is that the store's
      correctness rested on a property of a DIFFERENT package that nothing stated and
      nothing enforced, and it would be lost the day command handling is parallelised.
- [x] hub/internal/devices — the registry, whose Driver contract says methods are called
      concurrently and are NOT serialised. Refresh swaps the index while handlers read
      and actuate through it. Came through clean; the test catches a released write lock
      between the delete and the re-add (654k observed disappearances under that tamper).
- [x] controller/internal/transport — INVESTIGATED, no defect. Two goroutines write to the
      WSConn concurrently (the read loop's acks, the 2s event drain) and WSConn holds no
      write mutex. That looked like frame corruption waiting to happen. It is not: Go
      serialises concurrent Write calls on a net.Conn, so one Write per frame cannot
      interleave. Could not demonstrate corruption with four writers, 900 KB frames, a real
      TCP socket, or even a deliberately split write. NO test was kept — one that cannot
      fail is decoration — but writeFrame now states the invariant, because splitting that
      single Write would break it and nothing else enforces it.
- [ ] STILL UNCOVERED, measured: 10 more packages have concurrent production code
      (goroutines or a mutex) and zero `go func` in their tests. controller/internal/
      events is DONE (see below); the highest-consequence remaining is
      controller/internal/transport.
      Each needs a test shaped around ITS hazard, not a generic hammer — a shallow sweep
      Checked and found NOT to need one: controller/internal/state returns a copy from
      every reader under its lock (Pairing, Config and AccessPoints all copy), so there is
      no aliasing hazard to pin there.
      would restore the same false assurance being removed.

