- [ ] Modbus RTU (serial). TCP ships; a TCP-to-RTU bridge covers the common case.
- [x] The camera pipeline. All five steps of `docs/CAMERA-RETENTION.md` §4 are code:
      RTSP media (SETUP/PLAY/interleaved TCP), RFC 6184 depacketization, SPS parsing,
      access-unit assembly, an fMP4 writer, a recorder with per-camera retention and a
      free-space floor, an orphan sweep for files the index cannot see, a `camera:view`
      grant per member per camera with an audit row for every view AND every refusal,
      a clip list that renders its gaps, and MSE live view.
      WHAT REMAINS IS HARDWARE, and it is not a small remainder. Nothing here has met a
      camera: every test drives an in-process RTSP server, so a fake written from the
      same RFC agreeing with the code under test is still the risk on the wire format.
      Chromium ACCEPTS the container — a real independent check on the boxes, the avcC
      and the SPS — and never decodes a picture, because the fixture payloads are not
      decodable ones. And the retention arithmetic deletes real files under rules
      nobody has exercised on real footage. Recording is also one RTSP session per
      window rather than one held open: worse on a real camera, and honest about what
      has been verified.
