## phone linking (shipped 2026-07-28)
- [x] The chat rails were INERT on every real deployment and nothing said so. Every
      lookup in channels.go filters on `verified_at IS NOT NULL`, and the only writer
      of that column — store.AddVerifiedPhone — had test callers and no others. So no
      member could ever be recognised, and every WhatsApp/Telegram/Slack open was
      refused. Built, tested, documented as shipped, and dead.
- NOTE: this was an unfinished REPAIR, not an oversight. Invite-accept used to
      auto-verify the invited number (a squatting hole); the fix made invites link
      phones UNVERIFIED and removed the last production path that set the column. The
      replacement ceremony was designed in docs/PHONE-LINKING.md and never built.
      invites.go still promised "the OTP verify flow" — a flow that never existed.
- [x] Built it: migration 0018 phone_link_codes, store/phonelink.go, httpapi/phones.go
      (mint/list/unlink, session-only), and the channel side in phonelink_chat.go.
- NOTE: the security core is that a code is spendable ONLY by the number it was minted
      for. That is what makes minting safe without proof of ownership — which it cannot
      require, since proving ownership is the point of the ceremony. Tampered: removing
      the binding lets an attacker verify a victim's number onto their own profile.
- NOTE: redemption must run BEFORE the membership lookup, because someone linking is by
      definition not yet recognised. Pinned by a test that fails when the call is moved.
- NOTE: two deviations from the design, both recorded in the doc rather than silently
      resolved — the code hash is deterministic (the inbound message carries no selector
      to salt against, and six characters means the hash is not doing much work; the
      phone binding is), and the mint quota is per USER not per phone (per-phone hands
      an attacker a way to lock a victim out of their own number).
- [x] Telegram/Slack/Discord: SAME DEFECT, four more rails. channel_identities (0005)
      is how those rails resolve a member, and its only writer — LinkChannelIdentity —
      also had test callers and no others. So they were inert for the same reason.
      I recorded this as "unsolved and separate" an hour earlier; that was wrong. The
      same two facts exist on those rails (a code proves console access, an inbound DM
      proves control of the account), so migration 0020 runs the ceremony against
      channel_identities. Built + wired into all three rails.
- NOTE: the two ceremonies are NOT interchangeable, and this is the part worth
      remembering. A phone code names the number that may spend it, because the member
      knows their own number. Nobody knows their own Telegram numeric id — learning it
      is what the inbound message is FOR — so a channel code cannot name its target and
      whoever sends it gets bound. Phone code = 6 chars (possession of the handset is
      the second factor). Channel code = 12 chars / ~2^59 (there is NO second factor;
      the code is the only barrier). A guard fails if anyone shortens it, and the
      console copy tells the member not to share it.
- NOTE: three rails' unlinked prompts told members to "ask your admin to add <id> in
      the dashboard" — a dashboard feature that never existed, because nothing could
      write the table it would have written to. Now they describe the real ceremony.
- [ ] DMTAP deliberately gets NO linking ceremony: it is a scaffold with a fail-closed
      transport, and giving it one would imply a rail that works.

## the "built and unreachable" class — now found four times
- The shape: a correct, tested, carefully-documented component that NOTHING in production
      calls. Every existing check passes, because nothing is broken; the feature simply
      never runs. Found so far:
      1. Telegram long-polling — complete, tested, nothing constructed it.
      2. store.AddVerifiedPhone — the only writer of verified_at, test callers only, so
         no member could ever be recognised on WhatsApp.
      3. store.LinkChannelIdentity — same, for Telegram/Slack/Discord/DMTAP.
      4. energy.PruneSamples — retention written and guarded, never called, so the
         samples table grew forever on a Pi.
      Plus the sibling shape: the hub VERIFIED controller events and then dropped them.
- [x] A scan for it: exported *Store methods with no reachable caller outside
      internal/store, transitive (a per-file scan produces false positives — the audit
      hash-chain verifiers are reached via VerifyHashChains and are genuinely wired).
      After fixing the four above, the remaining list is short and triaged below.
- [x] The scan is now a CI guard: store/reachability_test.go, run by `go test ./...`.
      Transitive reachability, an allowlist where every entry carries a REASON, a floor
      that fails if the walk finds <100 methods, and a rot check so an allowlist entry
      naming a deleted or now-reachable method fails too. Tampered all three: a new
      unreachable method, a stale exemption, and a broken walk each fail by name.
- [x] Triaged the three it found. ControllerEventsByDevice was MINE and would have been
      the fifth instance, added by the same pass that catalogued the other four — now
      exposed at GET /v1/devices/{id}/events (admin-only, 404 for outsiders so a
      guessable device id leaks nothing). ExpireDeviceClaim and SetInviteTokenHash are
      genuine dev/test hooks whose own declarations say so and whose exposure would be
      a vulnerability (claim DoS; invite-credential replacement) — allowlisted with that
      reasoning. AddVerifiedPhone/LinkChannelIdentity/PhoneVerified are cross-package
      test fixtures over primitives production reaches through the ceremonies.
- [x] The FRONTEND twin of that guard: src/lib/__tests__/apiReachability.test.ts. routeParity
      proved every api.X call hits a real hub route; nothing proved a client method is ever
      CALLED by a screen. Same defect wearing frontend clothes — the hub serves it, api.ts
      wraps it, the docs describe it, and no screen offers it, so it looks finished from
      every angle except using it. Found 15; 9 are honest allowlist entries, 6 were real
      console gaps (automations delete/run-now/run-history, "log out everywhere", admin
      audit verify).
- NOTE: my first version of that guard put the allowlist-rot assertion AFTER the
      uncalled-methods assertion in the same `it`, so the rot check never ran while any
      method was uncalled — a check hiding behind another check's failure, which is the
      exact shape this whole effort keeps finding. Split into its own block and tampered.
- [x] The six real gaps the frontend guard found are closed: automations delete /
      run-now / run-history (an automations runtime you cannot inspect is one you cannot
      trust), "log out everywhere" (a session-security control that was curl-only), and
      the audit hash-chain verification — the whole point of chaining the audit tables is
      that "was this tampered with?" is a CHECKABLE question, and nothing in the console
      could ask it. Also added GET /v1/devices/{id}/events to the console for the same
      reason: routeCoverage caught that my new route had no client.
- NOTE: routeCoverage.test.ts already existed and checks hub-route → client; my new
      guard checks client → screen. Three links, three checks now: routeParity (client →
      route), routeCoverage (route → client), apiReachability (client → screen). I ran
      only the Go suite after adding the events route and missed routeCoverage failing.
- [x] railDisclosures now renders, in Settings directly under the two linking sections —
      where someone actually CHOOSES a rail, not in a doc they open afterwards. The hub's
      `note` is rendered verbatim and unconditionally: the four fields cannot say the
      thing most likely to be misread (self-hosting removes the middleman operator, not
      the platform), and a table without that sentence lets "you host it yourself" read as
      "nobody else sees your messages". runs_behind_cgnat comes from the hub, never
      re-derived locally. Both pinned by tests, both tampered.
- LESSON: my apiReachability guard could not see `api\n  .method()` — the regex needed
      `api.` contiguous. That was not a cosmetic under-report: it meant a method that HAD
      been wired up still looked uncalled, so the stale-allowlist check stayed quiet and
      the allowlist could widen silently. Worse, two of the entries I wrote reasons for
      (adminAccount, adminClaimState) were never uncalled at all — the admin screens have
      always called them in the chained style, so I had invented justifications for
      exemptions that should not exist. Fabricated exemptions are worse than none: they
      read as a decision somebody made. Fixed, all three removed, and reverting the regex
      now produces false alarms, which is how I know the fix matters.
- LESSON: api.ts typed logoutAll as returning {ok, revoked} and the hub sends {ok} only.
      Nothing read it yet, so nothing was broken — but the first person to render "N
      sessions ended" would have shipped "undefined".
- NOTE: the guard cannot see the SIBLING shape — a handler that receives something and
      discards it is perfectly "reachable". That was the controller-events defect, and
      nothing automated would have caught it.

## the class, fifth instance — on the controller this time
- [x] events.Queue.Compact: written, correct, with its own passing test, and called by
      NOTHING. The log is append-only, so a delivered entry leaves `entries` but its line
      stays on disk until the file is rewritten — which never happened. A controller that
      had delivered a million events still had a million lines, on the device in this
      system chosen for costing the least. Same shape as the hub's PruneSamples exactly.
- NOTE: the existing TestCompact proved the FUNCTION worked and asserted entry count
      after reopen — an assertion that passes unchanged even if the file never shrinks a
      byte. The new tests assert bytes, and one of them lives in package transport
      specifically to prove the CALL SITE exists, because that is the half that was
      missing and a function test cannot see it.
- [x] Two triggers: a one-off reclaim at Open (a controller power-cycled often would
      otherwise accumulate a run's worth of history per boot) and a threshold after
      drains. Open's failure is deliberately non-fatal — refusing to start because old
      lines could not be reclaimed would turn a full disk into a gate that cannot open.
- [x] Tried a controller-wide reachability guard and DELIBERATELY did not ship one. Two
      rules, both wrong: package-boundary flags every same-package helper (ws.go's six
      primitives are called from runner.go next door), and file-boundary with transitive
      closure reports ZERO because the declaration line itself matches a bare-name regex.
      The hub's version escapes that only because store methods are always called as
      `s.Method(` and it can require the leading dot; the controller mixes call forms, so
      the same trick does not transfer. A guard needing a 20-entry allowlist trains people
      to allowlist, which is worse than no guard.
- [x] Shipped the SHARP version instead: reclaimPathsAreWired.test.ts, spanning both Go
      modules. Two of the five instances were reclaim paths, and that is not chance —
      nobody misses reclamation until a disk is full, months later, on a machine nobody
      watches, so it is the category least likely to be caught by using the product.
      Matches Compact/Prune/Expire/Cleanup/Vacuum/Trim/Purge/Evict/Reclaim/Sweep/Rotate,
      one allowlist entry (ExpireDeviceClaim, a documented dev hook). Tampered: unwiring
      either real reclaim path fails by name, and breaking the pattern fails the floor.
- NOTE: it caught something immediately — splitting Compact into Compact/CompactIfNeeded
      left the unconditional Compact() dead surface AGAIN, an hour after fixing the
      original. Removed: reclamation happens at Open and past the threshold, both
      automatic, and a second entry point is just a second thing to forget to call.

## console surfaces for things the hub already served
- [x] engineHealth. Per-DRIVER health had no screen: the Devices page renders per-device
      state, and that cannot express a driver being down as a whole. An unreachable MQTT
      broker does not look like "the MQTT driver is down since 14:02" — it looks like a
      block of devices whose readings stop being fresh, which is indistinguishable from a
      quiet house. Now above the device list, rendering the driver's OWN detail string
      (the only line that says what to fix) and refusing to read an empty driver map as
      healthy. Both tampered.
- [x] energyChannels built rather than declined. The argument that settled it: the source
      MIX is derived entirely from per-channel `source`/`flow`, and those are ASSIGNMENTS
      somebody made, not measurements — a solar meter tagged as grid produces a chart that
      is confidently and invisibly wrong, with nowhere to look. The panel also surfaces
      `enabled` (a disabled channel contributes nothing, and its absence from the chart is
      indistinguishable from a meter reading zero) and the interval/gap-tolerance pair
      that produces the quality flags the screen already shows without saying where they
      come from. Read-only on purpose: editing a channel's source or scale rewrites what
      historical buckets MEAN, which is a decision with a paper trail, not a dropdown.
- NOTE: the api-reachability allowlist is now down to four entries, all genuine
      design decisions (refresh is redundant with the request interceptor; grant,
      accountsList and accountCreate have no console path by design). Every entry that was
      really unfinished work has been built.
- LESSON, twice now: a source-level guard can be satisfied by the component's OWN doc
      comment. The "contributes nothing" check passed against a version with the
      user-facing line deleted, because the phrase also appears in the file header —
      exactly the flaw the automations agent hit with `confirm(`. Assert a string that
      only the rendered JSX contains, and always tamper to confirm.

## the tier ladder's safety rule
- [x] "Stopping is never riskier than starting" was PROCEDURAL, not structural. Tier's own
      doc comment says the registry "refuses to build" without a safe inverse; nothing
      refused — checkInverses was reached only from a test, so the guarantee held exactly
      as long as somebody ran one. Same shape as bleperiph's ErrUnsupported sentinel and
      the ABI test's 32-bit claim: a comment naming an assertion the code never made.
      Now enforced at package init. Panicking is safe here and cannot surprise an
      operator: the catalogue is a compile-time literal with no dynamic registration, so
      a violation fires the first time any binary importing the package starts — in a
      terminal or in CI, never newly on a hub that worked yesterday.
- NOTE: the existing test asserted only that TODAY'S catalogue is clean, which a
      checkInverses that always returned nil would have passed just as happily. It proved
      the catalogue innocent and the checker nothing — and once init panics, it can never
      fail at all. Split checkInversesIn(cat) out so a test can feed it a deliberately
      broken catalogue: three cases (no inverse named, inverse absent from the capability,
      inverse itself above reversible — the subtlest, where an escape hatch exists but is
      no more reachable than the thing it undoes), plus a compliant catalogue that must
      still come back clean so the cases cannot pass against a checker that always
      complains.
- [x] docs/CHAT-COMMANDS.md said "Nothing in this document is built" while §1.2's closed
      verb set, §3's tier ladder and this rule had all been shipping. Header corrected to
      name exactly the two pieces that exist and to keep everything else PROPOSAL — the
      actuation Port seam of §1.4 still has no `actuate` package.
- [x] Verified §2.2's "four specific failures, all verifiable in the current code" against
      the code. ALL FOUR are fixed, and the doc still presented them as live — someone
      reading it would either re-fix fixed things or distrust code that is correct.
      (a) FindMentionedGate collects every hit and resolves only when unique; an ambiguous
      message actuates nothing and asks. (b) The cap is the shared PickerCapacity and a
      truncated menu carries TruncationNotice. (c) Slack's picker stops at the same
      constant instead of being unbounded. (d) ParseSelection returns ok=false with no
      prefix; the verb comes from a table, never the id's text. Each annotated FIXED in
      place rather than deleted, since the reasoning is why the code has its shape.
- NOTE: all four are pinned by tests already — disambiguation_test.go is organised around
      the defects by name — so the FIXED annotations cannot quietly become lies. Added a
      claim for the safety-relevant one, and confirmed the docRef quote is really checked
      by swapping it for a sentence the file does not contain.
- [x] §1.4's PREMISE was stale in a way that changes the design, not just the prose. It
      argued every non-access capability must return not_implemented BECAUSE the device
      engine did not exist. It does now — registry, four drivers, Execute, and a tier
      ladder that already classes every (capability, verb) pair — so a second
      implementation is honest where it was not: an EnginePort beside AccessPort.
      not_implemented stays a first-class outcome, just narrower than "everything except
      gates"; robot control still has no driver.
- [x] §1.4's not_implemented REPLY half shipped ahead of the Port, because it has a
      caller and the Port does not. "turn on the porch light" used to fall through to the
      welcome menu, so a member asking about a light was shown a list of GATES and an
      offer to open one. Nothing actuated — misleading, not dangerous — but the member
      could not tell "I did not understand you" from "that is not a thing I do", and only
      one of those is worth reporting to whoever runs the hub. Wired on all four rails.
- NOTE: the verb table maps typed words onto devices.Verb CONSTANTS, not a chat-side list,
      so it cannot describe capabilities the engine lacks. Matching is longest-phrase-first
      (deterministic, not map-order) and on word boundaries — "alarm" must not match arm,
      "sunset" must not match set. Both are tested; loosening to strings.Contains fires on
      both immediately.
- NOTE: deliberately does NOT touch the open path. A mixed body ("open the gate and turn
      on the light") still opens the gate, exactly as before. Refusing mixed intent is a
      real question but it is a change to the ACTUATION path, not to a fallback reply.
- [x] Checked whether AUTOMATIONS could be the actuate Port's caller. They cannot, and
      deliberately: the package doc says "'Open the gate at 07:00 every weekday' is not
      expressible here, on purpose", every access verb sits at TierPhysicalAccess, and
      MaxActionTier is TierConsequential — a line drawn because everything above it is a
      decision a person should be present for. Already well-tested (tier_test.go pins the
      ceiling AND makes anyone raising it say so out loud). So the Port still has no
      caller anywhere, and not building it remains right.
- [x] Pinned the automations package doc's THREE structural claims instead: no
      internal/store, no HTTP surface, narrow DB and Auditor seams. All were true and
      none were guarded — an import is one line somebody adds while fixing something
      else, and nothing in the build would notice: it would still compile, the tests
      would still pass, and "structurally cannot write to an audit table itself" would
      simply be false. That sentence is why an unattended actuator is allowed near an
      audit table at all.
- NOTE: widening Auditor breaks the BUILD first (every implementer stops satisfying it),
      so the type system is the stronger guard for the careless case. The arity check is
      for the careful one: a change that widens the interface and dutifully updates every
      implementer compiles perfectly, and nothing else would notice. My first tamper only
      "passed" because the build broke before the test ran — proved the mechanism by
      flipping the expected count instead.
- [ ] STILL NEXT: the `actuate` package. It is now a
      TWO-implementation seam rather than one, which is a bigger and better change than
      the doc describes. Build it with a caller in the same change — a Port with no
      consumer would be the sixth "built and unreachable" in a repo that has spent this
      long removing five.

## structural claims, held
- The technique that worked on automations generalises: a package doc saying "structurally
      cannot" / "the ONLY ones" / "by construction" is making a checkable claim, and those
      rot silently — the code still compiles, the tests still pass, the sentence is just
      false. Swept for them across both modules.
- [x] internal/automations: no internal/store, no HTTP surface, narrow DB and Auditor
      seams. All true, none guarded.
- [x] httpapi/server.go's API-TOKEN SURFACE. It claims exhaustively that four routes are
      the only ones a token can reach. TestAPITokenCannotReachUnscopedRoutes probes a
      dozen routes with a live token and is the right test of the MECHANISM — but it names
      its probes by hand, so a route that did not exist when it was written is invisible to
      it. The surface could grow with every existing test green. Now the route table itself
      is read and the set compared.
- NOTE: the second guard is the one worth having. A read scope guarding a mutating route —
      `tokenScoped(store.ScopeAccessRead, fence, s.handleSomethingCreate)` — reads
      perfectly naturally in review, and every existing token test still passes, because
      they check that the scope is ENFORCED rather than what it is attached to. That is a
      read-only token that can actuate.
- [x] store/authrecovery.go. The claim HOLDS — purpose is in the WHERE clause of the
      lookup, the claim and the invalidate, so a wrong-purpose token is not found at all.
      Guarded now (every statement must key on purpose; purpose must be BOUND, never
      interpolated). Worth pinning precisely because there is only ONE purpose today:
      no second flow exists to catch a regression, nothing would go red, and the property
      is being kept correct in advance of the thing it protects against.
- NOTE: its comment described "the two flows" and gave a username-verification token as
      the example — a token this hub cannot issue, since identity is a local username and
      migration 0009 leaves 'email_verify' out of the CHECK constraint deliberately.
      Corrected.
- [x] devices/modbus/config.go. The comment named TestReadOnlyCaps, "walks the catalogue
      and fails if any capability listed here has grown a verb above TierRead". THAT TEST
      DID NOT EXIST — third instance this session of a comment naming an assertion the
      code never made. The existing TestActuableCapabilitiesAreRefusedAtConfigTime covers
      the other direction only: a capability NOT in the allowlist. It cannot see this one.
- NOTE: the gap was real and non-obvious. readOnlyCaps is an ALLOWLIST, and "structurally
      read-only" rested entirely on every capability in it offering nothing but TierRead
      — a claim about a catalogue in another package that nothing tied to this one. A verb
      added to devices.CapMeter (a resettable counter, a calibrate) would have made every
      Modbus device actuable without a line of the driver changing, and the allowlist would
      still have read as correct. Fixed at construction, not just in a test: config
      validation now VERIFIES what the allowlist asserts, via a new devices.VerbsOf.
- [x] twofactor's shape claims. All three HELD and none were guarded: exactly one query
      selects user_totp.secret, TOTPStatus has no Secret field and its query does not
      select the column, and the recovery-code rows carry salt+digest but never the
      plaintext "which does not exist after issuance".
- NOTE: these are SHAPE tests on purpose. A behavioural test passes happily the day
      someone adds a second secret-selecting query for a perfectly good reason — which is
      exactly the change worth noticing. The secret is the whole second factor: anything
      that reads it mints valid codes forever, and unlike a password there is nothing to
      rotate that a user would notice. The reason the code uses a projection type with no
      Secret field rather than a struct that simply is not filled in is that the first
      cannot leak it and the second only has not yet.
- [x] Swept the CONTROLLER too — the half that actually opens gates, and which the hub
      sweep had not touched. Its highest-stakes claim is package pairing's: "the redeem
      response is the ONLY moment a gateway key is accepted; thereafter only a `repair`
      signed by the currently pinned key can change it — state.Store enforces that."
      TRUE, and the set was unguarded: one assignment, one SavePairing caller (pairing),
      one ApplyRepair caller (command, past the signature check).
- NOTE: ApplyRepair verifies NOTHING. Its doc says the caller must already have checked
      the envelope against the currently pinned key — a contract in a comment, honoured by
      exactly one call site. A second caller anywhere (a config reload, a recovery hook)
      rotates the trust root without a signature, and every existing test still passes.
      That is the takeover this guard exists for; tampering one in adds it to the list.
- NOTE: the scan deliberately includes BUILD-TAGGED files. A gpio- or ble-only file that
      wrote the pinned key is just as much of a takeover, and skipping it because the
      default build does is how a tagged file becomes the place to hide things.
- The structural-claims sweep is now COMPLETE across both modules: automations boundaries,
      the API-token surface, authrecovery's purpose key, modbus read-only, the TOTP secret,
      and the controller's key pinning. Six claims — five were unguarded truths, one
      (modbus) was a genuine hole.

## fail-closed claims, wired
- The structural sweep covered SHAPE. The controller also makes BEHAVIOURAL fail-closed
      claims, and those regress silently in a different way: the component keeps failing
      closed while its caller quietly stops caring.
- [x] noncestore: "a nonce that cannot be durably recorded is treated as unusable and the
      command is rejected", and NonceStore's interface repeats it — "any error must cause
      rejection (fail-closed)". The STORE honours it and has its own tests (full store,
      corrupt file). Nothing tested the wiring: that command.go acts on the error. It
      does, at line 108, and now a test injects a failing Mark and asserts the command is
      denied AND the relay never moved.
- NOTE: why it matters. Mark failing means the nonce is not on disk, and a disk write
      fails exactly when a power loss is imminent. The same signed bytes then replay after
      the reboot and the gate opens again. Tampering the check to `_ = ctx.Nonces.Mark(...)`
      returns Result:opened — the gate opening on a command with no durable record of it.
- NOTE: the test counts Mark CALLS as well, so it cannot pass for the wrong reason. Making
      the command expired instead refuses it earlier and fails with "Mark was never
      reached; this test proves nothing about the persistence failure" — which is what the
      first draft of a test like this usually does silently.
- [x] state.go's rollback claim was FALSE — a real defect, not an unguarded truth. Store's
      doc says without qualification that a mutation which cannot be persisted "returns an
      error and the in-memory state is rolled back". `prev := s.data` copies the STRUCT,
      which shares the map, the slice and the Pairing POINTER. Config was deep-copied
      explicitly; Pairing was not, and ApplyRepair mutates through the pointer. So a failed
      disk write during a repair rolled back everything EXCEPT the thing that had actually
      changed.
- NOTE: consequence. The controller keeps the new pinned key in memory while the disk
      still holds the old one; the hub gets an error ack, carries on signing with the old
      key, and the controller rejects it until a reboot restores agreement. A lockout, not
      a takeover — but from a guarantee documented as unconditional.
- NOTE: found by probing rather than reading. The comment said "rolled back", the code
      looked like it rolled back, and only running it against an unwritable directory
      showed the pointer sharing. Reading would not have caught this.
- NOTE, honest: of the three fields the new test asserts, only the pinned key actually
      discriminates. Lockdown is a value and was always fine; AccessPoints is replaced
      wholesale by its only mutator, so its snapshot is defensive for a future in-place
      mutator, not cover for a live bug — dropping it fails nothing today, and the test
      says so rather than implying three saves.
- NOTE: my own pinning guard then failed on the COMMENT I added explaining the fix, which
      contains the assignment as prose. Fixed the guard to skip comment lines rather than
      rewording the comment — a guard that counts prose invites whoever hits the false
      positive to weaken it. Verified a genuine second assignment still fails.
- [x] relay/gpio.go's "the only cleanup path that cannot be skipped". Better shape than
      expected: setCloexec already had a test, and a properly built one — it uses os.Pipe
      so it needs no gpiochip, and it CLEARS the flag first so it cannot agree with a
      setCloexec that does nothing. The call site is also already fail-closed: a failure
      closes the line and returns.
- NOTE: what was missing is the wiring, and it is the one thing no CI machine can ever
      run. The setCloexec call sits immediately after a GPIO_V2_GET_LINE ioctl, which
      needs a real gpiochip. So the function stayed proven and the CALL stayed unproven —
      invisible until a gate is held open by a process nobody can find, because an exec'd
      child inherited the fd and the kernel therefore never released the line.
- NOTE: a source-scan is a weaker check than running the code, and here the alternative is
      not a stronger check, it is NO check. Guards the call, that its error is branched on,
      that the line is closed on failure, and that nothing (SetFinalizer/KeepAlive) extends
      the line's lifetime past the fd's. Deliberately carries NO build tag, so it runs in
      the ordinary `go test ./...` rather than only when someone remembers -tags gpio.
- The fail-closed sweep is now complete: the nonce store's wiring, state.go's rollback
      (a real defect), and this. Three claims — one was false, two were true-but-unwired.

## bridge discovery told operators the wrong thing
- [x] KnownBridges lists zigbee2mqtt AND zwave-js-ui, but parseAnnouncement decodes ONE
      shape: zigbee2mqtt's retained JSON array on `<prefix>/bridge/devices`. zwave-js-ui
      does not publish that, so it was always classified SILENT — which the code's own
      comment defines as "subscribed to and stayed silent", i.e. we asked correctly and it
      did not answer. An operator reading that goes to check whether their bridge is
      running. It was running; we cannot read it whatever it publishes.
- [x] Added the third answer: BridgesUnreadable, plus a note telling the operator the
      devices are still reachable and to configure them by topic. "Did not answer" and
      "answered in a language we do not speak" send you to different places.
- NOTE: the existing test ENCODED the wrong classification — it asserted zwave-js-ui lands
      in BridgesSilent. Rewritten to cover all three states, including a genuinely silent
      readable bridge, so the distinction cannot collapse in either direction.
- NOTE: deliberately did NOT implement zwave-js-ui discovery. Its format is a configurable
      prefix with per-node topics, not a z2m-style array; writing a parser for a format I
      cannot verify against a real bridge would be guessing, and a wrong guess here
      proposes capabilities, which is how a tier gets assigned. Narrowing the claim is the
      honest move until someone has one to test against.
- README and site/docs said "bridge discovery reads your device list" right after naming
      both bridges. Corrected to name the one it reads.

## automations: the coupling that looks like an oddity
- [x] "Nothing is retried" holds WITHIN a run — TestIndeterminateIsRecordedAndNotRetried
      covers that. But the scheduler calls Fire again on the next occurrence, and the only
      thing between an unknown outcome and a second actuation is LastFiredAt being stamped,
      because MinIntervalS is gated on it. That coupling was untested.
- NOTE: it looks like an oddity, which is why it needed a test with the reasoning in it.
      "Only stamp LastFiredAt on success, since nothing was confirmed to have happened"
      reads as a correctness fix and is exactly the double-actuation the package doc exists
      to prevent. Tampering it that way fails with the reason spelled out.
- [x] Audited the other item-(1) drivers' claims while here. httpdev is SOUND and tested:
      a transport error before the request was written is unreachable, after it is
      INDETERMINATE, and a 5xx on a non-idempotent action is indeterminate too — with
      tests for each. Nothing to do, which is worth recording so the next pass does not
      re-audit it.
- [x] check:claims docRef matching now strips line-leading `//`, `*` and `>` before
      comparing. A quote from a comment that WRAPS became "... unknown // physical ..."
      once the newline collapsed, so it never matched and the only remedy was shortening
      the quote — which silently narrows what the reference pins. Hit three times in one
      sitting before fixing the tool instead of the quotes.

## spec-derived checks
The technique that found the 2^53 rounding: derive expected behaviour from proto/*.md
and check the code against it, rather than checking the code against itself. The vectors
cannot catch what both implementations get wrong together.
- [x] pairing.md's "`repair` must be signed by the CURRENTLY pinned key". Honoured —
      Process reads the pinned key fresh, verifies, and only then swaps. But only the
      HAPPY path was tested. Added the attack: a repair signed by an unrelated key, and
      one self-signed by the key it installs. The second is the shape a well-meant fix
      for the documented lost-key limitation would take, and it is a complete takeover;
      simulating that change makes the controller answer result=ok.
- [x] commands.md §4's "remembered until exp + skew (after which step 3 rejects it
      anyway)". Honoured — Mark's horizon and Verify's expiry bound align exactly, so no
      replay window exists. It was untested, and it is a coupling across two files that
      neither side's tests can see.
- NOTE: pruning is LAZY. Seen() checks membership only; entries are dropped inside
      Mark(), which a replay never reaches. So a sweep that only replays proves nothing —
      my first version did exactly that and passed against a deliberately broken horizon.
      The test now accepts a fresh command at each step, which is what triggers the prune
      and is also what a controller in use actually does.
- [x] grants.md's 11-step verification ORDER ("first failure wins"). Honoured,
      including the one apparent deviation — the cnonce lookup precedes step 3 because
      with no pending entry there is no grant to verify, and the code says so. Untestable
      by vectors, which are single-fault by design: five multi-fault cases now assert the
      EARLIER step wins. Verified that moving lockdown after the signature check leaves
      the whole vector corpus AND verify.mjs green while the new test fails.
- [x] events.md's delivery contract. The first of these audits to find a DEFECT
      rather than a coverage gap, and the largest one yet: the hub's handler for
      controller events was `s.log.Info("controller event")`. Verified, then dropped.
      Everything upstream of it was real — the controller signs each event, holds
      grant redemptions in a reserved partition that is never evicted, fsyncs an
      overflow log when that fills, and records a redemption BEFORE pulsing the relay
      (with its own test asserting that order). All of it delivered into a log line.
      Consequence: an offline emergency open, the ONE path with no hub in the loop and
      therefore the one nothing else records, left no trace on the hub. And events.md's
      "The hub dedupes on `event_id`" — the only mitigation the spec offers for its two
      acknowledged delivery gaps — was false, because nothing was stored to dedupe.
      Fixed: migration 0019 controller_events (event_id PRIMARY KEY = the dedupe),
      access kinds appended to the hash-chained access_logs with source=offline_grant,
      access point resolved from the DEVICE not the payload. Tampered all three:
      removing dedupe, letting the payload pick the AP, and restoring the original log
      line each fail a test naming the exact defect.
- NOTE: the ordering/at-least-once half of events.md needed no test. "Record before
      actuate" was already covered (TestOnRedeemedRecordsBeforeActuating), and the
      no-ack / no-sequence-number gaps are documented as v0-open in the spec, not
      claims. The claim worth checking was the one asserted in passing, mid-paragraph.
- [x] grants.md's revocation window. No defect and, unusually, no behavioural gap
      either: the section is almost entirely honest NEGATIVES ("only the grant's own
      exp bounds the exposure", "no message tells a controller a grant is revoked",
      v0-undefined), and the one lever it names — lockdown — is already covered by the
      vector corpus and by the multi-fault ordering test. What was unguarded is the
      STRUCTURAL claim underneath it: "the verification core takes no input besides the
      presented grant and local controller state". Two drift alarms now pin that — Env's
      exact field set, and no net/db/transport imports reachable from the core. Neither
      forbids the spec's own v1 revocation-list proposal; they fail naming the paragraph
      that must change with it, because an operator reads that paragraph when deciding
      how fast to latch lockdown after firing a resident. Tampered three ways: added an
      Env field, added a net/http import, and broke the file walk (the scan floor fired,
      which is the failure mode that made naming.test.ts vacuous).

## fuzzing
- [x] No fuzz target existed anywhere. Four now, on the two parsers that read
      UNAUTHENTICATED bytes: mDNS parseResponse/decodeName (any host on the LAN, one
      UDP datagram) and the LAN grant HandleOpen/HandleProof (any host on the LAN, one
      POST at a gate). ~5M executions by hand found nothing; 30s each in CI as a
      tripwire. Seeds run free in the ordinary suite.
- [x] controller/internal/framing (BLE frames from anyone in radio range). Found a
      real gap: Push returns a COMPLETED message alongside frame_too_large when a valid
      frame precedes an oversized header in one write. Safe, because blesession checks
      the error first — but nothing tested that ordering, and the existing
      frame_too_large test could not reach the case. Reversing two statements in the
      session issues a challenge on a connection being dropped for protocol abuse.
- [x] controller/internal/jcs — fuzzed for PROPERTIES, not just panics: idempotent,
      deterministic across map iterations, value-preserving, output re-parses. "Does not
      panic" is far too weak for the code that decides which bytes a signature covers.
- [x] hub/internal/keys JCS (the signing side). Same properties.
- [x] proto/jcs-cases.json — the two JCS implementations cannot import each other
      (Go's internal/ rule, and e2e depends on neither by design), so their agreement is
      asserted against SHARED DATA. Expected values derived from RFC 8785 by hand, not
      captured from either side. Found a real defect: 2^53+1 was silently ROUNDED to
      2^53 rather than refused, because Float64() lost the precision before the range
      check ran — in BOTH implementations. The documented deviation promises a refusal.
- [x] hub/internal/devices/modbus is now fuzzed: readADU (the wire frame), decodeReadResponse
      (the response-vs-request check) and Metric.decode (the numeric half). ~8M execs
      clean — the decoder was already careful, which is the honest result. The property
      worth having is Metric.decode's: a value that decodes successfully is FINITE, held
      over 1.6M random type/order/scale/offset combinations rather than the few cases the
      unit tests picked. Several inverters put NaN in a channel that is not measuring, and
      a NaN ingested as energy poisons every aggregate it touches — silently, because NaN
      compares false against every threshold anyone would check it with. Tampered: removing
      the finite guard fails on the seed corpus; loosening the length guard fails with a
      257-byte PDU the fuzzer built itself. All three added to CI.
- [x] CI now FUZZES the camera targets. They were absent from ci.yml's fuzz list — the
      H.264 pair since it was written, the RTSP pair from the day they landed — so their
      seed corpora ran under `go test` and no fuzzing happened in CI at all. The step's
      own helper already guards against a target that does not exist, because two JCS
