- [x] The camera RTSP text protocol is fuzzed (rtspfuzz_test.go, 2026-08-01):
      readRTSPResponse and parseSDP, the layer ABOVE the depacketizer h264_test.go
      already covered — where a camera's bytes first become structure. It found a real
      one within seconds: parseSDP let a control attribute keep an interior \r, and that
      value is interpolated into a request LINE, so `a=control:x\r\nFake: y` made the hub
      emit a SETUP it never composed. TrimSpace does not help; the injection is in the
      middle. Control characters are refused at parse time now, and the crasher is a
      committed regression seed. NOTE: a third fuzz assertion of mine was also wrong
      before the code was — I asserted status != 0 on success, and "RTSP/ 0" parses to a
      legitimate zero that every caller refuses one layer up.
- NOTE: two of my own fuzz assertions were wrong before the code was. The framing one
      forbade messages alongside an error; the JCS one compared numbers by SPELLING, so
      canonicalising `0e00` to `0` — exactly correct — looked like value corruption. A
      property assertion is a claim about the spec and deserves the same scepticism as
      the code.

- [x] A REAL RACE found 2026-08-01 by running `go test -race` locally on the packages
      a session had touched — something scripts/check.sh explicitly does not do
      ("the race detector ... CI-only"). TestAGrantedMemberWatchesLiveAndItIsAudited
      polled rec.Body.Len() while the handler goroutine wrote to the same
      httptest.ResponseRecorder, which is not safe for concurrent use. Its own comment
      said "a fixed sleep is a race dressed as a delay" — right about the sleep, and the
      thing that replaced it was a different race. Fixed with a mutex-guarded recorder
      wrapper (syncRecorder) plus Broadcaster.Viewers, which takes the broadcaster's own
      lock, to wait for attachment. Whole httpapi package now green under -race.

- [x] Swept the whole class after fixing it: THREE time.AfterFunc sites exist in
      production code, and only the one I fixed was wrong. GPIO's holdExpired takes
      the driver mutex and re-checks `g.state != StateHeld` before touching the line;
      Mock's pulse timer takes its mutex and re-checks "pulsing". Both are the
      check-and-act-under-one-lock pattern I had to arrive at by failing twice.
      Worth naming: the HARDWARE drivers got the harder version right, and the
      software layer above them called Stop() and assumed. command.go now points at
      them as the precedent it should have followed.
      Every other timer here is a channel timer read inside a select, which has no
      separate goroutine to outlive a cancel and cannot have this defect.

- [x] A REAL DEFECT on the actuation path, found by pulling on the concurrency open
      item. time.Timer.Stop() does not wait for a callback that has already started
      and returns false to say so; cancelRelease ignored the return, so after it
      returned the in-flight callback still called Relay.Release(). Measured before
      touching anything: 5 runs out of 5, cancelling at expiry.
      On a gate that reads: a hold is running, a new open arrives as it expires, the
      command path cancels and pulses — and the old callback closes the gate that was
      just opened, after the ack already said the open succeeded. A gate that shuts by
      itself and an audit trail that disagrees.
      Fixed with a generation counter. MY FIRST FIX WAS ALSO WRONG and -race said so:
      it checked the generation under the lock and released OUTSIDE it, so a cancel
      landing in between still let the release through. Check-then-act with the act
      outside the lock is not a check. Now the check and the release share one lock,
      and recording (which can fsync) stays outside it.
      MY FIRST TWO ASSERTIONS WERE ALSO WRONG: "no release ever" is unachievable —
      a callback that reached the relay before cancel took the lock genuinely
      happened first. The honest property is that nothing further arrives once
      cancelRelease has RETURNED, so both tests snapshot at that instant. Two tampers
      CAUGHT (no generation at all; check-outside-lock), 20 iterations per run,
      6 runs under -race, whole controller race-clean.

- [x] controller/internal/events now has the concurrency test its own open item asked
      for, and it is there because I ADDED a goroutine to that package's callers last
      week: the held_open watcher records events while the command path, grant path
      and listeners do the same. I had race-tested that watcher against a FAKE
      recorder holding its own mutex, which proves nothing about the real one.
      Reading the code says it is safe — Enqueue takes q.mu, Synced.Now takes its
      own, NewEventID is crypto/rand, Recorder is immutable after construction. The
      test is the evidence, and it asserts more than "no race": every event must
      survive (a lock-protected queue can still overwrite a slot) and every event id
      must be distinct, since the hub dedupes on it and a collision would drop a real
      event as a redelivery — neither is visible to the race detector.
      MY FIRST FIXTURE MISSED HALF ITS SUBJECT. Removing the CLOCK's mutex was NOT
      CAUGHT, because NewSynced(0, nil) leaves it unsynced, so Now() returns
      time.Now() and never reads the shared fields. Added a goroutine that keeps
      calling SyncFromGateway — which is what the agent does on the hub connection —
      and both locks are load-bearing now: queue lock removed -> CAUGHT, clock lock
      removed -> CAUGHT.

