- [x] BUILT held_open, the gate-left-open event proto/events.md had reserved since
      the beginning. Yesterday established the missing piece was not a sensor — the
      GPIO driver has one — but EDGE WATCHING: a poll answers "closed now", an alert
      needs something looking more than once. That is now controller/internal/agent/
      heldopen.go, behind `-held-open-after` (0 = off), started only when the relay
      also implements Sensors and one is actually present. The hub needed NO change:
      RecordControllerEvent stores any kind verbatim, which I checked before writing
      anything.
      The honest part is the payload. `seconds` is how long the sensor has NOT
      REPORTED CLOSED, which is not "the gate was open that long" — GateClosed
      returns (false, true) both when the gate is open and when the line cannot be
      read, deliberately, and the two are indistinguishable through that interface.
      An operator who reads "not reported closed for 5 minutes" and finds the gate
      shut has learned something true: the sensor is broken.
      Fires ONCE per episode and re-arms on a closed reading — the event queue is
      durable and at-least-once, so a repeat-every-tick alert would fill it with
      copies of one fact. Three tampers CAUGHT (no re-arm, repeat-every-tick, the
      absent-sensor branch). Race detector clean over 3 runs.
      MY TEST WAS RACY AND THE CODE WAS FINE: it advanced the fake clock before the
      watcher's first tick, so the "not closed since" stamp landed 30s late and the
      threshold was never reached. Instrumented the watcher directly rather than
      re-reading my own logic. Two earlier tampers were also junk — one did not
      compile (unused variable), one hit a fast path whose real guard is the
      per-tick check.

