- [x] The audit chain cannot see its own TAIL being deleted, and four documents said
      the ceiling was the expensive attack. README/SECURITY/ARCHITECTURE and
      audithash.go's header all described the honest limit as "edits a row and
      recomputes every hash forward" — true, and it reads as "the cheap attacks are
      caught". They are not: deleting the most RECENT rows needs no hash work at all,
      every surviving row still points at its true predecessor, and the walk returns
      OK. Measured before writing anything: 5 rows -> delete 2 -> OK=true,
      RowsChecked 5->3. Middle deletes ARE caught (prev_hash mismatch).
      Nothing inside the file can fix it — an anchor beside the rows is editable by
      whoever edited the rows — so the verify result now carries Head, the CLI prints
      `(N rows, head <64 hex>)`, and GET /v1/admin/audit/verify returns it. A count
      alone is NOT an anchor: ordinary activity refills it. Docs now say all of this.
      TWO of my own tamper runs were worthless before they were useful: the first
      used `go build` as the detector for a missing PRINT (of course it passed), and
      the honest re-run showed NOT CAUGHT — no test asserted the CLI prints the head,
      while three documents had just started telling operators to record it. Added
      that test; the tamper is caught now, as is making both chains share a genesis.

