## race detector
- [x] Never run on this repo before. Run by hand across hub, controller and e2e, plus
      the gpio and ble tagged builds: CLEAN, no data races anywhere. Now a CI job so it
      stays that way.
- NOTE: hub/internal/httpapi takes ~18 min under -race because every register/login
      derives argon2id at 64 MiB. The first run FAILED at 608s — that was the default
      600s `go test` timeout, which reports exactly like a race. Timeouts are set
      explicitly in the job. The hashing parameters are deliberately not lowered for
      tests.

## relay safety
- [x] The MaxHold watchdog bounded ONE latch, not continuous energised time. Repeated
      `hold` commands re-latched after each expiry — measured 396ms continuously
      energised against a 150ms bound. Fixed with a cooldown equal to MaxHold. Found by
      investigating a "flaky" test instead of re-running it: the test sampled once at
      the end and landed in the brief de-energised gap about a third of the time.
- LESSON: a flaky test on a safety behaviour is the worst combination — it gets re-run
      until green and then trusted. Sample throughout and assert on the worst case.
- [x] Swept the relay tests for the same shape. Three slept a fixed time into a running
      pulse and then acted; the sleep began before the pulsing goroutine was scheduled,
      so the real margin was never what the test appeared to allow. They wait for the
      observed state now. Checked Pulse for the Hold defect too — MinPulse/MaxPulse are
      documented as bounding ONE pulse, and repeated pulses are the rate limiter's
      concern, so there is no unmet claim there.

## drift guards — audited for vacuous passes
The Go side too: bleperiph/platform_test.go's filename-constraint guard had the same
"look at every file, report offenders" shape and passed on zero files. Its sibling test
was worse — its comment named the assertion that matters (the no-backend build must
return exactly ErrUnsupported, because agent.go treats anything else as fatal and would
stop the controller) and the code only logged. Both fixed; BackendLinked is now a
compile-time constant per backend so a test can tell a stub build from a real one.
Every source-scanning test was checked by breaking its extraction and seeing whether it
noticed. naming.test.ts did NOT: four of its five guards passed with walk() returning
nothing, including the one keeping the gateway→hub rename from regressing. Fixed with a
floor on every walk. safetyCopy, responseShape and routeParity all fail correctly —
their assertions are "this is present" or they carry explicit sanity floors.
- The shape to watch: "collect offenders, assert the list is empty" passes perfectly on
  an empty scan. "assert this sentence is present" fails safely. Prefer the second, and
  where the first is necessary, assert the scan found something first.

## second copies of hub-owned vocabularies — swept
The console restates several lists the hub owns. Found three by accident (metric hints
13 names behind the drivers; capability→controls complete only by luck; rail disclosure),
so the rest are now pinned by tests: comparison operators (a closed <select>, so drift
makes an engine feature unreachable), automation trigger kinds (engine AND schema CHECK),
access-point kinds (handler AND schema CHECK), API token scopes, device kinds, wire
constants, refusal reason codes.
- The asymmetry that matters: a value the console offers and the hub rejects is VISIBLE.
  A value the hub accepts and the console never offers is INVISIBLE — no error anywhere,
  the option simply is not there.
- LESSON: a source-scanning guard must strip comments first. Commenting an option out of
  a picker left its literal in the file and the check passed on a picker that had lost
  an entry.

## §26.3 disclosure — swept, all four rails
Three of the four declared a configuration-dependent property unconditionally, and each
time the existing guard AGREED with the false claim. Now mode-aware, with a generic
endpoint test that fails if a moded rail stops passing config through.
- Telegram: webhook by default, outbound only with AQL_TELEGRAM_ENGINE=polling.
- Slack: webhook by default, outbound only when SLACK_APP_TOKEN is set.
- WhatsApp: the bridge engine changes price AND initiation (it can cold-call).
- Discord: genuinely unconditional here, but the Interactions Endpoint URL lives in
  Discord's settings and this hub cannot see it — stated in the note instead.
- LESSON: site/docs/reachability.md had Slack right all along. The prose doc was more
  accurate than the machine-readable table, which is the opposite of the usual
  assumption.

## console ↔ hub drift (tracked by src/lib/__tests__/routeParity.test.ts)
CLOSED. routeParity's KNOWN_UNAVAILABLE list is empty — every route api.ts calls, the
hub serves. Adding an entry back is a real decision, not bookkeeping.
- [ ] Phone linking is the one that was NOT a missing route: the hub has owned
      phone→member mapping since 0002 and requires verified_at to grant access, but
      nothing has ever verified a number, and both obvious shortcuts let an attacker
      squat a neighbour's number. Design + the squatting hazard: docs/PHONE-LINKING.md.
      Migration 0018 is unclaimed and reserved for it.
- Not doing: Google OAuth, and any email-based auth. The hub is meant to work off nodes;
  recovery is username-based through a delivery seam, not an email dependency.

## api & integrations
- [ ] Reachability is guarded for `*Store` methods and for the whole controller module,
      and for NOTHING ELSE in hub/. Measured 2026-08-01 with a planted-orphan control:
      311 exported declarations under hub/internal outside store/, 31 with no production
      caller. Most are legitimate — test constructors, package API another package uses —
      but two were not, and both were found only because the scan was run by hand:
      `accessdev.NewAccessDriver`, a one-line alias to `New` with no caller anywhere, and
      `channels.OccupancyEnabledButUnbuilt`, a carefully-argued reply for a limitation
      that no longer exists ("which lights are on" is built, CHAT-COMMANDS.md §4.2).
      Both deleted. 3 of the remaining 31 are now classified, all legitimate and all
      now SAYING so at their declaration — which is what a future allowlist needs and
      what none had: keys.VerifyCommand is the CONTROLLER's verifier carried in the hub,
      the mirror of the controller carrying wire.VerifyWSAuth, so proto/vectors checks
      two independent implementations rather than one agreeing with itself;
      keys.SignCommand is the plain signer the vectors and e2e use, production going
      through the rotation-aware wrappers; devices.TierOf is a fail-safe alternative to
      Supports()+ok that nothing needs and that costs nine lines.
      Then four more in channels/, all superseded wrappers rather than dead ends, all
      now annotated: Disclosure/Disclosures return the DECLARED table and ignore the
      Config, so on a polling hub they would publish a Telegram transport claim that is
      false — production must use DisclosureFor/DisclosuresFor, and the tests keep the
      raw pair because they assert properties of the declaration itself; TextGateVerb
      collapses TextGateIntent's command-vs-QUESTION distinction to a bool, so a rail
      using it would answer "is the gate open" by opening it; GateVerbForCommand is the
      bare string mapping under SelectionCommandVerb. Then four in camera/ —
      MediaFlow.Intact, StreamInfo.VideoResolution, Fragmenter.Params and
      Fragmenter.DecodeTime — all accessors that let a test observe pipeline state
      without exporting the fields, which is a design choice rather than a defect and
      is now said ONCE in camera/doc.go instead of four times at the declarations. That
      pipeline has never seen hardware, so tests are the only caller it has ever had.
      Then recording/: EnsureFreeSpace was "exported for the worker" and no worker
      exists — the internal form runs before every WriteClip, which is the right place
      because that is where space is consumed, so the export was deleted and the tests
      (same package) call the unexported one. Broadcaster.Viewers claimed to exist "for
      the capture loop to decide whether producing live output is worth anything", an
      optimisation the design forbids: fragments are published from inside WriteClip by
      the same muxer that writes the file, so a viewer and the disk see identical bytes,
      and the output exists whether or not anyone watches. Doc corrected.
      Then the rest, all legitimate: Weekdays and the mock driver's AddDevice/Drop/
      NewMockDriver are fixture constructors; IsNotFound is an errors.Is wrapper;
      DenialReasons says at its declaration that it exists so a test can assert every
      reason has a message; UnmarshalJSON is called by encoding/json; energy Mix.Share
      is a convenience the API deliberately does not use — /v1/.../energy/mix returns
      coverage_seconds and expected_seconds per source, so a client can show the GAP
      rather than a boolean "exact"; camera's BackwardsTimestamps and
      MarkerDisagreements are diagnostics of the same kind as the four already covered
      in camera/doc.go. One was NOT legitimate: GateQuestionReply, deleted.
      SWEEP COMPLETE. Of 33 uncalled exports: 3 deleted, 2 missing entry points built
      (gen-data-key, mqtt-scan), several docs corrected, the rest classified in place.
      GUARD WRITTEN, from those verdicts rather than in bulk:
      devices/hubreach_test.go. hub/ now has the coverage store/ and controller/ had.
      The reason it could not be written in one sitting was
      exist — an allowlist written in bulk is the thing store/'s guard warns against.
      A signing-path symbol sat outside every guard for four commits; see the keys note.
- [ ] a token-scoped `GET /v1/events` feed (today the audit log is instance-admin only)
- [ ] revoke-a-controller endpoint (no DELETE on /v1/devices yet)
- [ ] API docs with python/go/curl examples

## conformance
- [x] store/tenants.go's six superseded methods are gone; the tenancy-scoping test now
      drives the accessors production uses.
- [x] ~32 uncalled exported symbols swept. Two of the six with NO caller at all
      (not even a test) turned out to matter: NewTelegramPoller was an entire unwired
      feature, and jcs.CanonicalizeJSON was an unrun entry point in a safety-critical
      package. The four left are UnmarshalJSON (encoding/json calls it) and three
      energy functional options. RESOLVED: all three were seams for behaviour nothing
      tested (the poller's clock on untimed readings, the per-device read timeout, the
      rollup budget), each confirmed by tampering first. They earn their place; they now
      have callers. UnmarshalJSON is called by encoding/json and is fine.
      CORRECTION (2026-08-01): "they now have callers" was wrong for FIVE, and the
      entry also undercounted — there are four energy options in this position, not
      three. WithClock, WithReadTimeout, WithRollupBudget and
      WithCounterGapInterpolation have no PRODUCTION caller; the hub constructs the
      store and poller and takes every default. Only WithLocation is set in anger.
      WithClock says "For tests." and is fine; the other three are real tunables with
      no config surface reaching them, which is a different thing and now says so at
      each declaration — the missing piece is a config field, not the option.
      And it was wrong for one more:
      keys.CanonicalizeJSON is reached only from tests, and jcs.go's own comment
      claimed four production callers by naming it alongside Canonicalize, which
      genuinely has them. The comment now separates the two and says why the
      test-only one stays. Neither reachability guard covers package keys, which
      is how a symbol in the SIGNING path went four commits without one.
- [x] TypeScript sweep RE-RUN 2026-08-01 with a planted-orphan control, after the Go one
      found two unreachable features. 215 exported declarations, 8 used nowhere outside
      tests, and no third finding: the two wire constants are pinned by
      wireConstants.test.ts (which the scan skips, being under __tests__),
      verifyAqlWebhook is sample code shown to integrators inside ApiReference.tsx,
      and countryByCode / isAlwaysOpenWindow / canonicalMinusSig / resetHubReachability
      are helpers their own tests exercise. One was worth the look:
      selectGrantForAccessPoint picks among SEVERAL grants for one hub, and the vault
      keys records `${hubPubkey}::${memberId}`, so a device holds at most one — the
      page's lookup by hub is correct under that model rather than a shortcut past this
      function. Said at its declaration, with what to do if the vault ever stores more.
      No TS guard written: unlike Go, the honest candidates here are mostly test-pinned
      constants and documentation samples, so the rule would be noise.
- [x] Same sweep run over TypeScript (exported VALUES with no use anywhere, counting
      in-file use — the Go version's lesson). 4 candidates from 302 exports: two false
      positives (sample code inside a CodeBlock template literal; a type-only export),
      one unused component (deleted), and one real find — src/lib/offline/grant.ts
      restates four wire constants in TS with nothing pinning them to the contract.
      Now guarded by wireConstants.test.ts against proto/vectors' spec_constants AND
      both Go declarations.
- LESSON: "no caller" is a question, not a verdict. Of the six symbols with no caller at
      all, one was an unwired feature, one an unrun entry point in a safety-critical
      package, three were untested seams, and one was a false positive. Delete last. Most are legitimate —
      test helpers, functional options, build-tagged constructors, encoding/json
      interface methods — but the sweep is worth re-running after any big change. The
      first version of it reported ZERO because a function's own doc comment counts as
      a use; strip comments before counting.

